{"text": ".\nNevertheless, I think Kopete would have been better as a plugin for Gaim itself, with a focus on integration with KDE and Qt. I have nothing against the Qt toolkit, but in fact if there's no way to write a Gtk+ client for it, that's a pretty big limitation.\nIf you read the Kopete announcement you'll find the following:\n<i>\"If you already have Gaim and Libpurple installed, a new icon will show up in KDE's \"Internet\"-folder. Or you can start Kopete directly from the command line.\"</i>\nWell, in fact I tried that. I had the latest Gaim and Libpurple from Fedora Core 2, and after building and installing Kopete I found that none of the IM protocols were available. The only options were \"None\" or \"IRC\". So much for all the plugins.\nI suppose there are other ways of obtaining the Libpurple library, but in fact I don't know much about it, but just took the RedHat version as a test. The plugin interfaces are also \"beta\". But given that there's been some time for Kopete to be stable, the problem remains. They have failed to integrate with the base IM library", "doc_id": 1}
